1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:02,357
Capy is a food log you talk to.

2
00:00:02,357 --> 00:00:04,122
You say what you want to eat.

3
00:00:04,122 --> 00:00:07,651
Capy finds a meal that still fits today, and that you will actually eat.

4
00:00:07,651 --> 00:00:09,164
The log fills in after that.

5
00:00:10,244 --> 00:00:11,575
People want to stay healthy.

6
00:00:11,575 --> 00:00:13,705
They need a clear picture of the day.

7
00:00:13,705 --> 00:00:15,302
Logging food is part of that.

8
00:00:15,302 --> 00:00:17,431
Most apps make logging harder than the meal.

9
00:00:17,431 --> 00:00:18,230
A search box.

10
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:18,762
A barcode.

11
00:00:18,762 --> 00:00:21,158
A meal plan that is gone in a week.

12
00:00:21,158 --> 00:00:23,820
You miss a day, and it feels like you failed.

13
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,879
What can I eat right now, with what I have left?

14
00:00:27,879 --> 00:00:29,233
That is the real question.

15
00:00:29,233 --> 00:00:30,858
A tracker gives you a form.

16
00:00:30,858 --> 00:00:34,921
You stop, look up each food, guess the portion, and hope the number is right.

17
00:00:34,921 --> 00:00:37,900
If you only ate half, there is nothing real to change.

18
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:41,617
You say the meal, or you ask what fits.

19
00:00:41,617 --> 00:00:45,718
Capy looks the food up, turns your portion into grams, and writes one line.

20
00:00:45,718 --> 00:00:48,062
The calories and macros come from that food.

21
00:00:48,062 --> 00:00:51,284
Capy tells you what it logged, and what you have left.

22
00:00:52,364 --> 00:00:54,210
Logging a meal takes three steps.

23
00:00:54,210 --> 00:00:56,979
Capy cannot skip one and make the plate up.

24
00:00:56,979 --> 00:00:58,210
It finds the food.

25
00:00:58,210 --> 00:01:01,594
It turns your portion into grams, using that food’s own measures.

26
00:01:01,594 --> 00:01:03,133
Then it writes one line.

27
00:01:03,133 --> 00:01:06,210
The numbers for 100 grams are copied from the food.

28
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:07,748
The log does the math.

29
00:01:07,748 --> 00:01:11,748
If you only ate half, Capy opens today and changes that one line.

30
00:01:12,828 --> 00:01:16,279
When your hands are busy, you say the meal to Alexa+.

31
00:01:16,279 --> 00:01:18,474
It uses the same tools, through MCP.

32
00:01:18,474 --> 00:01:21,298
If you are not signed in, nothing is logged.

33
00:01:21,298 --> 00:01:26,003
On the phone, Capy waits until you confirm the food, the grams, and the numbers.

34
00:01:26,003 --> 00:01:27,258
The model can talk.

35
00:01:27,258 --> 00:01:29,140
It cannot make up the meal.

36
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:32,608
Alexa+ and the phone share one log.

37
00:01:32,608 --> 00:01:34,654
API Gateway is the front door.

38
00:01:34,654 --> 00:01:39,088
App Runner runs the server and writes the meal while you are talking.

39
00:01:39,088 --> 00:01:42,499
Lambda runs the phone and waits for you to confirm.

40
00:01:42,499 --> 00:01:43,864
Bedrock is the model.

41
00:01:43,864 --> 00:01:45,228
DynamoDB stores the log.

42
00:01:45,228 --> 00:01:46,592
Cognito signs you in.

43
00:01:46,592 --> 00:01:48,980
The USDA key stays in Secrets Manager.

44
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:52,086
The meal comes in through the gateway.

45
00:01:52,086 --> 00:01:56,139
The tools find the food, turn the portion into grams, and write the line.

46
00:01:56,139 --> 00:01:57,876
The phone shows the same log.

47
00:01:58,956 --> 00:02:01,108
Two large eggs and a cup of rice.

48
00:02:02,438 --> 00:02:05,238
That is what got logged, and this is what you have left.

49
00:02:06,568 --> 00:02:08,360
I only ate half.

50
00:02:09,690 --> 00:02:10,439
Same day.

51
00:02:10,439 --> 00:02:11,938
That one line changed.

52
00:02:13,268 --> 00:02:14,724
The phone is the same log.

53
00:02:15,804 --> 00:02:16,508
You eat.

54
00:02:16,508 --> 00:02:18,268
The log is already written.

